cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  agent-evidence-admission: reference admission rails for agent execution
  evidence
message: >-
  This is software. It implements admission policies over the
  adversarial-execution-evidence predicate; the normative artifact is that
  predicate's own text, settled upstream in the in-toto attestation project.
  This repository carries no standing as a standard or a specification of its
  own. Cite the specification for what the predicate requires, the conformance
  vectors for what a corpus forces, and this repository only for what a
  particular policy engine was measured to enforce. Every disposition here is
  pinned to a corpus digest, so a claim taken from a branch rather than from a
  released version is a claim against a moving target.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Gilda
    given-names: Sankalp
    email: sankalp.gilda@gmail.com
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3645-4501'
repository-code: https://github.com/probityai/agent-evidence
abstract: >-
  Four policy engines that admit or refuse a Kubernetes workload on the in-toto
  adversarial-execution-evidence predicate, each one declaring per obligation
  whether it enforces the obligation, only approximates it, or cannot reach it
  at all. The dispositions are measured rather than asserted: every rail is run
  over every vector of the published conformance corpus, and a rail that
  declares an obligation enforced while answering differently from the oracle on
  a vector citing that obligation is a hard error. The rails are the incumbent
  admission controllers, Open Policy Agent, Kyverno on both its JMESPath and its
  CEL surface, and sigstore policy-controller with CUE, so a deployment adds a
  policy rather than a component.
keywords:
  - admission control
  - policy as code
  - attestation
  - conformance testing
  - in-toto
  - kubernetes
  - open policy agent
  - kyverno
  - sigstore
  - agentic systems
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.2.0
date-released: "2026-10-26"
